   <title>Dysbiosis of a microbiota–immune metasystem in critical illness is associated with nosocomial infections</title>
   <authors>Schlechte J, Willing B, Lowes D, West ML, Mager DR, Fuhr JE, Gold MR, Bhatt M</authors>
   <journal>Nature Medicine</journal>
   <year>2023</year>
   <study_design>prospective longitudinal cohort</study_design>
   <population>Adult ICU patients, University of Calgary, Canada</population>
   <sample_size>51</sample_size>
   <paper_summary>This prospective cohort study of 51 critically ill ICU patients used 16S rRNA sequencing of rectal swabs to characterize gut microbiome dynamics during the first week of critical illness. Enterobacteriaceae relative abundance was markedly elevated (~10-fold) in ICU patients compared to healthy controls and dominated compositional shifts over time. Progressive Enterobacteriaceae enrichment was independently associated with increased odds of nosocomial infection (OR 6.8) and with correlated innate immune dysregulation including elevated proinflammatory cytokines and immature neutrophil expansion.</paper_summary>
  </Paper>
